<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muş</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mush</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muş, also spelled Mush, is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, capital of Muş Province, on a fertile plain west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a>. It was the centre of the historic Armenian district of Taron, held by the Mamikonian and later Bagratid princes, with the great monastery of Surb Karapet nearby. Its Armenian population was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of 1915.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mus.jpg" alt="Muş" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mush_1213.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1366 Shaykh Uways Jalayir defeated their leader Birdi Khwaja at Mosul and his brother Bayram Khwaja at the battle of <b>Mush</b>, and from about 1375 the Qara Qoyunlu were Jalayirid vassals ruling from Mosul.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1890 the London paper The Era described the melody as written over a hundred years before, and it appears to have been adapted from the folk song &quot;<b>Mush</b>, Mush&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/botany-bay">Botany Bay</a></li><li><blockquote>Taron, the plain around <b>Mush</b>, had long been a Mamikonian land.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-taron">Principality of Taron</a></li></ul></section>
